<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">6 March in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 6 March, from 1204 to 1957.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1204</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Philip II of France takes Château Gaillard</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">French troops captured Richard the Lionheart's great castle above the Seine after a six-month siege, some climbing in through a latrine chute. Normandy fell to France within months.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1820</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">President Monroe signs the Missouri Compromise</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Missouri was to enter the Union as a slave state and Maine as a free one, with slavery barred from the rest of the Louisiana Purchase north of 36°30'. The balance held for three decades.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1836</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Alamo falls to Santa Anna</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Mexican troops stormed the mission at dawn after a 13-day siege, killing nearly all of its roughly 200 defenders, including Jim Bowie and Davy Crockett. 'Remember the Alamo' became the Texan battle cry.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1857</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">US Supreme Court decides Dred Scott v. Sandford</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Chief Justice Roger Taney ruled that Black Americans could not be citizens and that Congress could not ban slavery in the territories. The decision deepened the crisis that led to civil war.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1930</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Clarence Birdseye's frozen foods go on sale</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Shops in Springfield, Massachusetts, began selling quick-frozen vegetables, fruit, fish and meat using Birdseye's process. Frozen food went on to transform shopping and diets.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1957</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Ghana becomes independent</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Gold Coast became independent as Ghana under Kwame Nkrumah, the first sub-Saharan colony to win independence from a European power after the war. It inspired independence movements across Africa.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a></div></li></ol>
